@database "okf"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: okf.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "OKF knowledge bundles"
@{h1}OKF knowledge bundles

Agents forget; a knowledge bundle does not. This plugin lets agents read,
search and write curated knowledge kept as plain markdown files in the
Open Knowledge Format (OKF v0.1): a runbook for a host, the conventions of a
code base, what a tool is for and how it relates to others. Because a bundle
is only a folder of text files, you can read and edit it yourself, keep it
in git, and hand it to other OKF tools.

A @{b}bundle@{ub} is a folder. Every @{tt}.md@{utt} file in it is a
@{b}concept@{ub}: a YAML header with at least a non-empty @{tt}type@{utt},
then a markdown body. Concepts link to each other with ordinary markdown
links (@{tt}[Customers](/tables/customers.md)@{utt}), and those links make the
bundle a graph an agent can walk. @{tt}index.md@{utt} and @{tt}log.md@{utt}
are reserved: an overview and a change history, not concepts.

The plugin has nine tools and one hook, which folds the concepts that fit
the question into the conversation before each model call. It has no panel.
Tools and hook only touch folders inside the directories the server entry
allows.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The format  " link format}
  @{"  Agents that use it  " link agents}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Finding concepts: list, search  " link find}
  @{"  Reading a concept  " link read}
  @{"  The link graph: neighbors, subgraph  " link graph}
  @{"  Writing a concept  " link write}
  @{"  Log and index  " link logindex}
  @{"  Checking a bundle  " link validate}
  @{"  The context hook  " link hook}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node format "The format"
@{h1}The format

A concept is a markdown file with a YAML header between two @{tt}---@{utt}
lines:

@{code markdown}
---
type: Runbook
title: Restart the backup job
description: What to do when the nightly backup hangs.
tags: [backup, storage]
---

# Steps
Check the [storage host](/hosts/storage.md) first ...
@{body}

@{h2}Header fields

@{table}
Field | Meaning
type | required, any non-empty text; the only hard rule of the format
title, description, resource, tags, timestamp | optional; title and description are what lists and indexes show
status | the lifecycle: @{tt}draft@{utt}, @{tt}stable@{utt} or @{tt}deprecated@{utt}; missing or empty means stable
anything else | kept as it is; the plugin never drops a key it does not know
@{body}

@{tt}deprecated@{utt} is how OKF retires knowledge: the concept stays for its
links and history, and every tool that shows it marks it as retired. Case
and spaces around the value do not matter. A @{tt}status@{utt} holding
something else (@{tt}publishable@{utt}, @{tt}true@{utt}, a date) reads as
stable everywhere and is reported by @{" the check " link validate}.

@{h2}Paths and links

Concept paths are relative to the bundle, with a leading slash:
@{tt}/hosts/storage.md@{utt}. Every tool also takes them without the slash.
A link in the body is an edge of the graph when it is an inline markdown
link @{tt}[text](target)@{utt} whose target is:

@{bullet}@{tt}/hosts/storage.md@{utt} -- bundle-relative;
@{bullet}@{tt}./other.md@{utt} or @{tt}../hosts/x.md@{utt} -- relative to the concept's own folder;
@{bullet}either with an anchor, @{tt}/hosts/x.md#disks@{utt} -- the anchor is dropped.

Images (@{tt}![alt](...)@{utt}), web addresses, @{tt}mailto:@{utt} and bare
@{tt}#anchors@{utt} are not edges. A path written in plain prose is invisible
to the graph.

@{h2}Reserved files

@{tt}index.md@{utt} in any folder and @{tt}log.md@{utt} in any folder are
never concepts: the write tool refuses them, list and search do not show
them. The root @{tt}index.md@{utt} may carry @{tt}okf_version@{utt} in its
header; the list tool reports it. The file @{tt}.okf.lock@{utt} in the
bundle root is the plugin's write lock (see @{" Writing a concept " link write}).
@endnode

@node agents "Agents that use it"
@{h1}Agents that use it

@{table}
Agent | How
okf_agent | curates and answers from bundles with the @{tt}okf@{utt} tools; asks which bundle when you name none
sysadmin | its own instance @{tt}sysadmin_okf@{utt}, sandboxed to @{tt}data/okf/infra@{utt}, with the hook
coder | its own instance @{tt}coder_okf@{utt}, sandboxed to @{tt}data/okf/coder@{utt}, with the hook
@{body}

The shared instance @{tt}okf@{utt} allows all of @{tt}data/okf@{utt} but
the bundles of the agents with an instance of their own (@{tt}infra@{utt},
@{tt}coder@{utt}, @{tt}gamedev@{utt}, @{tt}n8n@{utt}): an agent with its tools can write every other bundle
there, including one another agent gets injected into its prompt. Where
that matters, give the agent an instance of its own whose
@{tt}allowed_directories@{utt} is only its bundle, and list that bundle in
the shared instance's @{tt}excluded_directories@{utt}, as is done for those
four -- then the sandbox keeps the boundary, not the prompt. An own
instance alone keeps the agent in, not the others out.

Bundles are not separated by user: whoever runs an agent with the tools
reads and writes the same folders.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
okf:
  type: okf
  enabled: true
  allowed_directories:
    - data/okf
  excluded_directories:
    - data/okf/infra
    - data/okf/coder
    - data/okf/gamedev
    - data/okf/n8n
  # hook_bundle: data/okf/<bundle>
  hook_max_concepts: 6
  hook_graph_depth: 1
@{body}

Allow the tools in an agent's list:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+okf/*"
@{body}

The hook is switched on per agent, see @{" The context hook " link hook}.

@{h2}Server settings

Written directly in the entry or under a @{tt}config:@{utt} block; when both
name a key, the block wins.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
allowed_directories | the data directory's @{tt}okf@{utt} | the folders bundles may lie in; a relative path counts from the project root
excluded_directories | none | folders inside those that this instance may not touch, because another instance owns them; a bundle inside one, or containing one, is refused
read_only | false | the three write tools are not offered to the model, and refuse when called anyway
max_concept_file_kb | 512 | a larger file is skipped by list, search, check and hook; the read tool returns at most this much text per call
max_bundle_files | 5000 | only the first this-many @{tt}.md@{utt} files (sorted by path) are loaded; the rest is ignored without notice
hook_bundle | none | the bundle the hook uses; without it the hook does nothing
hook_max_concepts | 6 | at most this many concepts per hook block
hook_graph_depth | 1 | link hops the hook follows from its seeds
hook_seed_count | 3 | search hits the hook starts from
hook_seed_concept | none | a fixed starting concept instead of a search
@{body}

@{h2}Where data lives

Bundles are folders under the allowed directories, in this repository
@{tt}data/okf/<bundle>/@{utt}. The write tools create a missing bundle or
folder. Each write also leaves @{tt}.okf.lock@{utt} in the bundle root
(Windows removes it again, other systems do not); add it to
@{tt}.gitignore@{utt} if you version a bundle.

@{h2}Dependencies

@{tt}ruamel.yaml@{utt} (keeps the header's order, comments and quoting on
rewrite) and @{tt}filelock@{utt}, both declared in @{tt}plugin.toml@{utt}.
@endnode

@node find "Finding concepts: list, search"
@{h1}Finding concepts

Every tool takes @{tt}bundle@{utt}, the bundle's folder: absolute, relative
to the project root, or @{tt}data/...@{utt}. It must lie in an allowed
directory and neither inside nor around an excluded one; a path naming
another host (@{tt}//host/share@{utt}) is refused before anything is
opened. The examples use the sysadmin's bundle, which only
@{tt}sysadmin_okf@{utt} reaches.

@{h2}okf_list

The overview: every concept with its header fields.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
dir | only concepts under this folder, @{tt}/hosts@{utt} or @{tt}hosts@{utt}; missing: the whole bundle
@{body}

Answer: @{tt}status@{utt} "ok", @{tt}bundle@{utt}, @{tt}count@{utt},
@{tt}version@{utt} (the root index's @{tt}okf_version@{utt} or null) and
@{tt}concepts@{utt}, sorted by path, each with @{tt}path@{utt},
@{tt}type@{utt}, @{tt}title@{utt}, @{tt}description@{utt} and
@{tt}lifecycle@{utt}. At most 200 concepts are listed; @{tt}count@{utt} is
the full number, and beyond 200 the answer adds @{tt}omitted@{utt} and a
@{tt}hint@{utt} to narrow with @{tt}dir@{utt} or search. A bundle folder that
does not exist lists nothing.

@{h2}okf_search

Ranks concepts by the words of a query.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
query | required; words are letters, digits and underscores in any script, compared in lower case
limit | 1 to 50, default 8; a value outside is moved to the nearest end, 0 or missing means 8
@{body}

A concept's text is its title, description, type, tags and body. The score
adds, for every distinct query word the concept contains, how often it
occurs times log(1 + concepts / (1 + concepts containing it)): rare words
count more. Only concepts with a score above 0 are returned, best first,
equal scores by path. There are no stems or synonyms: "backup" does not find
"backups".

Answer: @{tt}query@{utt}, @{tt}count@{utt} and @{tt}results@{utt}, each with
@{tt}path@{utt}, @{tt}score@{utt} (4 decimals), @{tt}type@{utt},
@{tt}description@{utt} and @{tt}lifecycle@{utt}.

@{code json}
{"bundle": "data/okf/infra", "query": "backup hangs", "limit": 3}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "ok", "query": "backup hangs", "count": 1,
 "results": [{"path": "/runbooks/backup.md", "score": 4.1589,
              "type": "Runbook",
              "description": "What to do when the nightly backup hangs.",
              "lifecycle": "stable"}]}
@{body}

@{h2}Errors of every tool

Answers with @{tt}status@{utt} "error" and @{tt}error@{utt}:

@{table}
error | When
missing 'bundle' path | no bundle given
bundle '<bundle>' is outside the allowed OKF directories | not in an allowed directory, inside or around an excluded one, or on another host
@{body}
@endnode

@node read "Reading a concept"
@{h1}Reading a concept

@{tt}okf_read_concept@{utt} returns a concept's header and body. Any file in
the bundle can be read this way, @{tt}index.md@{utt} and @{tt}log.md@{utt}
included.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
path | required; the file, with or without leading slash
start_line | first body line, from 1; smaller values mean 1
line_count | how many body lines; missing or 0: all from start_line on; negative: 1
@{body}

Line numbers count the body only, without the header. A final line break
is not a line.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
path | as asked
frontmatter | the header with every key, or null without a header or when it cannot be parsed
parse_error | why the header could not be parsed, else null
body | the body, or the asked lines of it
lines_total, line_start, lines_returned | where the slice lies
lines_remaining, hint | only when lines follow the slice
@{body}

The hint names the next @{tt}start_line@{utt} and warns not to write the
slice back as the body -- the page would be cut to it:
"35 more line(s) — continue with start_line=16. Do NOT write this slice
back as 'body': that cuts the page to it."

Without @{tt}line_count@{utt} the whole body comes back. An answer is at
most @{tt}max_concept_file_kb@{utt} (512 KB by default); a larger page, such
as a long log, is read in slices.

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
error | When, and what else comes with it
missing concept 'path' | no path
concept path '<path>' escapes the bundle | the path leads out of the bundle, or names another host
concept not found: <path> | no such file; " — did you mean '<other>'?" when a concept's path is close. Also @{tt}did_you_mean@{utt} and @{tt}available@{utt}, the concepts in the same folder
the text asked for is larger than <n> KB — read fewer lines with start_line/line_count | the slice (or whole body) is over @{tt}max_concept_file_kb@{utt}; with @{tt}lines_total@{utt}
start_line=<n> is past the end: the page has <m> line(s). Nothing read. | start beyond the last line; with @{tt}lines_total@{utt}
@{body}

No suggestion is made when the paths differ only in a number: a missing
@{tt}step_15.md@{utt} beside @{tt}step_16.md@{utt} is a gap, not a typo, and
the listing in @{tt}available@{utt} shows it. A file that is not UTF-8 text
fails the call.
@endnode

@node graph "The link graph: neighbors, subgraph"
@{h1}The link graph

@{h2}okf_neighbors

The concepts one concept links to.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
path | required; with or without leading slash
@{body}

Answer: @{tt}path@{utt}, @{tt}neighbors@{utt} (existing concepts in the
order the links appear, each once) and @{tt}broken_links@{utt} (targets that
are neither a concept nor @{tt}index.md@{utt}/@{tt}log.md@{utt}, as often
as they occur). An unknown path answers "concept not found" with
@{tt}did_you_mean@{utt} and @{tt}available@{utt}, as the read tool does.

@{h2}okf_subgraph

Everything reachable from one or more starting concepts within a number of
link hops, following links outward.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
seeds | a list of concept paths
seed | one path, used when @{tt}seeds@{utt} is missing or empty
depth | 0 to 5, default 1; a value outside is moved to the nearest end
@{body}

Answer: @{tt}seeds@{utt} (with leading slash), @{tt}depth@{utt},
@{tt}concepts@{utt} in breadth-first order, the seeds first, and
@{tt}count@{utt}. A seed that is no concept is left out without an error;
with none left the answer is empty.

@{code json}
{"bundle": "data/okf/infra", "seed": "/hosts/storage.md", "depth": 1}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "ok", "seeds": ["/hosts/storage.md"], "depth": 1,
 "concepts": ["/hosts/storage.md", "/runbooks/backup.md"], "count": 2}
@{body}
@endnode

@node write "Writing a concept"
@{h1}Writing a concept

@{tt}okf_write_concept@{utt} creates a concept or changes one.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
path | required; must end in @{tt}.md@{utt} and may not be @{tt}index.md@{utt} or @{tt}log.md@{utt}; missing folders are created
frontmatter | required, an object
body | the markdown body; missing or null: a new concept has none, an existing one keeps its body; "" empties it
@{body}

@{h2}Changing a concept

The given header is merged into the existing one: a key given replaces the
old value, a nested object is merged key by key, a key set to null is
removed, every other key stays -- with its comments, order and quoting.
Lists are replaced, not merged. So retiring a concept needs no body:

@{code json}
{"bundle": "data/okf/infra", "path": "/runbooks/old-backup.md",
 "frontmatter": {"status": "deprecated"}}
@{body}

A body that is sent replaces the old one whole. Never send a slice read
with @{tt}start_line@{utt}/@{tt}line_count@{utt}.

After merging, the result must have a non-empty @{tt}type@{utt}; otherwise
nothing is written. The file is written to a scratch file first and then
put in place, so nobody ever reads half a concept.

@{h2}The answer

@{tt}status@{utt} "ok", @{tt}path@{utt} as given and @{tt}bytes@{utt}, the
length of the written text in characters.

@{h2}Errors

@{table}
error | When
OKF server is read-only | @{tt}read_only@{utt} is set
'<path>' is a reserved OKF filename (index.md/log.md) — use <instance>_append_log / <instance>_reindex | a reserved name
concept path '<path>' must end in .md | anything but a concept file
'frontmatter' must be an object with at least a 'type' | no object
frontmatter is missing a non-empty 'type' field | no type after merging; also @{tt}findings@{utt}
another writer is holding the OKF bundle lock (> 30s) — try again | see below
@{body}

@{h2}Several writers

The three write tools read a file, change it and write it back. Two writers
at once would lose one change silently, so each write holds two locks for
the bundle: one inside the process (parallel tool calls and sub-agents) and
the file @{tt}.okf.lock@{utt} for other processes (the API, workers, the
command line). A writer that cannot get them within 30 seconds gets the
lock error instead of hanging the turn.
@endnode

@node logindex "Log and index"
@{h1}Log and index

@{h2}okf_append_log

Adds an entry to a folder's @{tt}log.md@{utt}.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
dir | the folder whose log it is; missing: the bundle root
date | required, a real date @{tt}YYYY-MM-DD@{utt}
time | @{tt}HH:MM@{utt} or @{tt}HH:MM:SS@{utt}, a real time; missing: see below
action | the entry's short label, default "Update"
description | what changed; links are allowed
@{body}

Without @{tt}time@{utt} the server stamps the current time -- but only when
@{tt}date@{utt} is today in the timezone of @{tt}context.timezone@{utt} in
@{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} (UTC without it), the same zone the agent's
own date comes from. An entry for an earlier day gets no time at all rather
than a wrong one.

@{code markdown}
# Update Log

## 2026-09-30
* 14:03:07 **Update**: Disk layout of [storage](/hosts/storage.md)
* 09:12:44 **Creation**: Backup runbook

## 2026-09-28
* **Deprecation**: Old backup runbook
@{body}

A new log starts with @{tt}# Update Log@{utt}. An entry goes directly under
its date heading, so the newest comes first; a new date gets its heading
before the first older date, and after all of them when it is the oldest.
Headings that are no date are left where they are.

Answer: @{tt}path@{utt} of the log, @{tt}date@{utt} and @{tt}time@{utt}
(null when none was written). Errors: "'date' must be YYYY-MM-DD", "'time'
must be HH:MM or HH:MM:SS", "'dir' escapes the bundle", and the read-only
and lock errors of @{" Writing a concept " link write}.

@{h2}okf_reindex

Writes @{tt}index.md@{utt} from the concepts' descriptions.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
bundle | required
dir | one folder only; missing: the whole bundle
heading | the heading of the index; without @{tt}dir@{utt} only the root's
@{body}

With @{tt}dir@{utt}: that folder's @{tt}index.md@{utt}, listing the concepts
directly in it, not deeper; the heading defaults to the folder name with a
capital. Without: one @{tt}index.md@{utt} in every folder that holds
concepts at any depth, each listing its own concepts and linking the index
of every subfolder with the number of concepts below it; the root heading
defaults to "Contents", the others to the folder name.

@{code markdown}
# Contents

* [hosts](/hosts/index.md) - 4 concept(s)
* [runbooks](/runbooks/index.md) - 7 concept(s)
@{body}

A line reads @{tt}* [name](/path.md) - description@{utt}; a retired concept's
description begins with @{tt}[deprecated]@{utt}. An index is written anew:
text added by hand is lost, only the header of an existing index (such as
@{tt}okf_version@{utt}) is kept.

Answer: @{tt}path@{utt} (the index of @{tt}dir@{utt} or of the root),
@{tt}entries@{utt} (concepts counted) and @{tt}indexes@{utt} (files
written).
@endnode

@node validate "Checking a bundle"
@{h1}Checking a bundle

@{tt}okf_validate@{utt} checks every concept of a bundle. It takes only
@{tt}bundle@{utt}.

@{table}
rule | Severity | Found when
frontmatter-parseable | error | the header is no valid YAML, or no mapping
frontmatter-required | error | the file has no header
type-required | error | @{tt}type@{utt} is missing or empty
status-vocabulary | warning | @{tt}status@{utt} is set to something other than draft, stable or deprecated
link-resolves | warning | a link leads to no concept and no index or log
@{body}

Answer: @{tt}concepts@{utt} (the number checked), @{tt}conformant@{utt}
(true without errors), @{tt}errors@{utt} and @{tt}warnings@{utt} (counts),
and @{tt}findings@{utt}, each with @{tt}path@{utt}, @{tt}rule@{utt},
@{tt}severity@{utt} and @{tt}message@{utt}.

Files the loader skips -- over the size cap, beyond
@{tt}max_bundle_files@{utt}, unreadable or not UTF-8, or symbolic links pointing out of the bundle --
are neither checked nor reported; the server log names them.
@endnode

@node hook "The context hook"
@{h1}The context hook

@{tt}okf_context_injection@{utt} runs before every model call of an agent
that switched it on. It finds the concepts that fit the conversation and
appends them to the history, so the model has the bundle's knowledge
without calling a tool.

@{h2}Switching it on

In the agent's YAML, with the server instance's name in front:

@{code yaml}
hooks:
  enabled: true
  overrides:
    sysadmin_okf.okf_context_injection:
      enabled: true
      hook_bundle: data/okf/infra
      hook_max_concepts: 5
@{body}

Every setting of the table in @{" Setting it up " link setup} that starts
with @{tt}hook_@{utt} can stand here and wins over the server entry. The
bundle must lie in that instance's allowed directories.

@{h2}What it does

@{bullet}It takes the last message a person wrote -- a user message with text that no loop or hook added.
@{bullet}With @{tt}hook_seed_concept@{utt} set and present in the bundle, that concept is the only seed and the message is not used; otherwise the seeds are the best @{tt}hook_seed_count@{utt} search hits for the message (at most @{tt}hook_max_concepts@{utt}).
@{bullet}From the seeds it follows links @{tt}hook_graph_depth@{utt} hops outward and keeps the first @{tt}hook_max_concepts@{utt} concepts.
@{bullet}It renders them as one block and appends it to the end of the history as a developer message, marked as coming from @{tt}okf@{utt}.

The block:

@{code text}
[OKF knowledge context — relevant curated concepts]

## Restart the backup job (Runbook) — /runbooks/backup.md
# Steps
Check the [storage host](/hosts/storage.md) first ...

## storage (Host) — /hosts/storage.md  [DEPRECATED — retired, do not treat as current]
...
@{body}

The header line has the title (the path without one), the type
("concept" without one) and the path; the body follows, cut after 1,500
characters with @{tt}…[truncated]@{utt}. Header fields are not included.

@{h2}When it does nothing

@{bullet}no @{tt}hook_bundle@{utt}, or one the instance may not reach (see @{" Setting it up " link setup});
@{bullet}the bundle holds no concepts;
@{bullet}no seed: no person's message and no seed concept, or no search hit;
@{bullet}the block would be the same as the last block it appended.

An error inside the hook is logged as a warning and the model call goes on
without a block. The hook has 5 seconds.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Tool and parameter descriptions come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, the names prefixed with the instance's name
(@{tt}okf_search@{utt}, @{tt}coder_okf_search@{utt}). The nine tools are
about 7,400 characters in every request of an agent that has them. The
schema's limits are not checked by the framework; the tools enforce them
themselves, as described in each node.

@{h2}The status line

@{table}
Tool | The chat shows
list | "4 concept(s) under /hosts/ in data/okf/infra", with ", first 200 listed" when cut
search | "Searching <bundle> for '<query>'", then "'<query>': 3 hit(s) — best /x.md (4.1589)" or "(searched N concepts)"
read | "read /x.md — 2345 chars", with ", lines 1-40 of 120" for a slice and ", type=Runbook"
neighbors | "/x.md: 2 neighbor(s)", with ", 1 broken link(s)"
subgraph | "5 concept(s) within depth 1 of /x.md"
write | "created /x.md — 812 bytes, type=Runbook" or "updated ..."
append_log | "logged to /log.md — 2026-09-30 14:03:07 Update"
reindex | "regenerated 3 index file(s), 11 concept(s) -- <bundle>"
validate | "<bundle>: 11 concept(s), 0 error(s), 2 warning(s) — conformant"
error | the error text
@{body}

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary results appended to the history. The hook
appends too: its block is a new message at the end, the history before it
stays byte-identical, and the provider's prompt cache holds. Within one
question the block is the same on every step and is written once. A new
question that selects other concepts appends a new block; the earlier ones
stay where they are, so a long session carries every block it was given --
at most @{tt}hook_max_concepts@{utt} times about 1,600 characters each.
A block that compaction removed comes back at the next call.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Search is by words only; there is no semantic search.
@endnode
